description:
`secret.txt` was made by mirroring the alphabet: `A` swaps with `Z`,
`B` with `Y`, and so on. Mirror it back and read the flag.

Flag format: NCTF{...}

`secret.txt` holds only UPPERCASE letters `A-Z` and the digits `2-7`,
ending in a run of `=` padding. Decode it and read the flag.

Flag format: NCTF{...}

`roster.csv` is a small spreadsheet of players. One cell, among all the
ordinary notes, holds the flag. Open it and find it.

Flag format: NCTF{...}

`backwards.txt` contains the flag written from right to left. Read it
the other way round.

Flag format: NCTF{...}

`secret.txt` reads almost like the flag, but every letter has been
rotated halfway around the alphabet. Rotate it back and read the flag.

Flag format: NCTF{...}

`cipher.txt` was enciphered with a Vigenere cipher. The key is not a
secret this time -- it is `WARMUP`. Only the letters were shifted;
digits and punctuation were left alone. Decrypt it and read the flag.

Flag format: NCTF{...}

All we recovered is one line of dense ASCII (`cipher.txt`). Work out what it really is and recover the flag it leads to.

Stuck? Hints are available (they cost a few points).

Flag format: NCTF{...}

On vous fournit un package-lock.json et les archives .tgz qu'il verrouille. Le lockfile epingle une empreinte d'integrite pour chaque dependance, mais une des archives livrees a ete alteree. Trouvez-la : ce qui y a ete ajoute -- la cause exacte de l'ecart -- porte le flag, mais il n'est ni en clair ni en base64, il faut le reconstruire.

Stuck? Hints are available (they cost a few points).

Flag format: NCTF{...}

`system.txt` starts with a `CIPHER` blob, then lists 160 lines of three
integers `a b m`, each stating that `a * x` leaves remainder `b` when divided
by `m`. Every line has exactly one solution `x` in `[0, m)`. Solve them all;
together they unlock the cipher.

Stuck? Hints are available (they cost a few points).

Flag format: NCTF{...}
